WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Today's show is sponsored by new Findus Toilet Wallets!

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.000
We're not sure what they are either.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.540
We sell 'em, you smell 'em!

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.680
You just open it:
[Mechanical door noise]

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.740
This is the Technical Difficulties.
We are playing Citation Needed.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.220
I have an almost-randomly selected article from

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.340
everybody's favourite source of knowledge, Wikipedia,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.160
in front of me, and these folks cannot see it.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.840
Every fact that they get right is a point and a ding [DING],

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.880
and there is a prize for particularly good answers which is...

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.900
And your topic today is: 'simplehuman'.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:34.740
[Laughter]

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.400
Hello!

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.000
Hi!

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.860
Pass a bigger shovel, we're going to be digging deep today.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.100
I use the cardinal directions of 'up', 'down', 'left' and 'right'.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.580
I think I qualify.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.440
Is it like the whole Simple English thing, or something?

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.860
Oh, like a simpler version of the language?

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.100
Yeah, is it sort of an overall thing to try and

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.180
achieve that in humanity or something, or...?

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:00.920
No.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.160
Is it an album?

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.140
Ooh, no it's not, but that's a nice prog rock album title there.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.260
I was going to say, 'Simple Human' is a...

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.980
That is pretty much Granddaddy.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.820
Yeah, I thought it would be something like that. Okay...

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.200
Let's get the obvious — is it an LP,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.780
is it a film, or something like that?

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:18.740
Is it media? Is it a medium?

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.300
It's not media at all.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.840
Okay. Is it some kind of eco-revolution?

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.540
Ooo, not really, no.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.900
Oh sure, like a Good Life thing.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.920
A notion about how mankind used to exist...

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.920
pre-evolutionary or something like that?

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.780
You're all thinking really quite big-picture here,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.160
and not at all commercial.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.100
— Commercial?
— Commercial.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.700
Oh, do they make bog roll?

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.560
Oh, you know what, you're getting a point. [DING]

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.760
What!

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.360
It's not that close...

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.580
They make rubbish bins,

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.460
but that's close enough out of the blue

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:49.920
that I'm going to...

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.720
That's like standing in a pub with a dart in your hand,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.120
spinning around three times with your eyes closed,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.680
doing THAT and getting a bulls-eye!

00:01:58.820 --> 00:01:59.980
That's how I play darts.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.300
And that's why you're banned!

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.060
Oh, actually yeah, I've seen Matt play darts.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.600
Flailing!

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.700
They go through to the other side and into the wall.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.200
Actually, to be fair, it's more like standing in a pub

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.400
with a dart in your hand, a pint in the other hand,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.800
throwing at the dartboard
and knocking everything down in the skittle alley.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:17.720
Yes. Yes.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.320
So simplehuman make bins.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.700
They do. They are considered, and I quote here,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:29.100
'among the most high-end [trash] cans on the market today'.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.700
Can you tell me why?

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.240
Why are they some of the best bins?

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.720
They wear a cravat.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.800
Are they antibacterial or er, micro... biobial...

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.380
— Micro-biober...
— Miroberber...

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.380
There were some words there, Matt. And the answer:

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.600
No.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.820
Higher —
Are they made of gold?

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.720
Or diamonds? Or something otherwise desirable?

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.220
I'll give you a point for diamonds, because it's...

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.100
Carbon!

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.400
...durability. [DING]

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:53.900
Durability? They last a long time.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.000
It's one of the reasons.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.480
Huerrr...

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.760
I don't know what that noise meant.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.420
I can't count the number of times I've broken my bins.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.420
No, you literally can't, because it's zero.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:02.880
— Yes. [Laughs]
— Yeah.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.920
Yes. I've never broken a wheelie bin or anything in my life.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.860
I mean, I've ridden in one, and I've never broken it.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.120
Are we talking dumpster-styley things, though,

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.840
— Yeah, actually...
— that get serious industrial...

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.120
No, we're talking much...

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.920
— Kitchen.
— Yeah, kitchen bins.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.060
An unbreakable kitchen bin?

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.840
Do they do the solar compacting thing?

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.660
Are they odour-proof?

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.140
Odour-suppressant?

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.040
Do they play a nice tune when you put something in them?

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.160
['Shave and a haircut' melody]

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.560
Do they make the tea?

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.060
I seem to recall a giveaway...

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.900
I feel like it was some soft drink or other,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.700
that gave you a little thing that you attached to your bin

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.180
that made a noise every time you opened it.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:38.380
What was the noise though?

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.380
All right, you come home late from the pub one night...

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.660
If you're in my situation — you're a married man,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.980
you've snuck back in, not wanting to wake the wife up,

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.760
you've taken your shoes off, you've done the lot...

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.920
You've sneakily eaten the kebab

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.500
that you said you weren't going to have,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.880
you tiptoe over to the bin...

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.540
[Comedy music riff]

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.060
At this point, it's good you've taken your shoes off,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.580
because you're sleeping on the couch tonight.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.200
Yeah. 'Where've you been?!'
That kind of thing.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.560
Great. Thanks for making a noisy bin-thing.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.780
You're right about technologically advanced bins here,

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.460
but it's one thing in particular that it does.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.520
Techno-Bins!

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.120
No, that's just bins that dance to repetitive music.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.640
[Dance beat]

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.360
This is not a product placement,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.360
this is just what 'Random Article' pulled up for me.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.420
Does it sift and sort and do your recycling for you?

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.740
No, it's much less advanced than that.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.500
Does it play 'Free Bird'?

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.300
Does it let you put rubbish in it?

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.600
— No.
— Yes. Easily.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.040
What would make that more —

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.160
Front flap!

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.160
How more easy than an open-mouthed...

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.760
It's not open-mouthed.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.620
It's got an automatic lid.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.700
— Bingo! Point to you. [DING]
— Ahh.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.860
It has an automatic sensor-activated lid.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.000
— I think I've seen adverts for that now.
— Yup.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.380
— What...
— Like, late night, on a silly channel

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.700
that no one ever watches anyway.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.940
So what, you wave at it? Play a trumpet?

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.240
Call it?

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.400
There is just no hope left for humanity, is there really?

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:57.560
No, not really.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.820
This is basically, you wave your hand toward the bin

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.580
and it opens for you.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.540
Until of course it breaks down, at which point it just becomes...

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.820
But they don't break! It says.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.700
What happens when the bin turns against you

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.620
and starts throwing banana peels in your face?

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.480
...which will happen!

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.960
When the monkeys take over the world, anyway.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.080
What else have they added sensors to?

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.480
Because there's other kitchen and bathroom stuff

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:17.960
they've added sensors to.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.420
Oh. Knives. I've seen sensors in knives.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.380
[Laughter]
Whoa, whoa — what?!

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.380
Forks! Sorry, no...

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.140
It wasn't a knife, it was a fork.
[Laughter]

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.100
Oh God. Curry tonight's going to be entertaining, isn't it.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.960
[Caveman grunts]

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.200
What would you do to a fork? What possible...

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.480
When you're cooking a steak.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.640
— Ah...
— It's got a temperature probe on it,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.980
and it tells you when it's done.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.520
'Bring the temperature probe...'

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.640
So surely you just need a knife and a temperature probe.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.840
Why, when you could have it in a fork

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.080
that you can then use to eat it?

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.320
Or something, I don't know.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.740
But it was a thing you stuck in a steak.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.760
There was another... a pan!
They put a sensor in a pan as well.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.080
— What for?
— Steak, again!

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.860
— Okay.
— I've seen it on a shopping channel.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.880
Of a pan that will tell you

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.020
of what manner your steak will be done...

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.740
— ...at the current temperature of pan.
— Oh, okay.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.660
My big issue with this is that Brannan watches shopping channels.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:07.620
Of course I watch shopping channels!

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.360
He's a married man. What else has he to do?

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.580
Occasionally, we go down the shopping channels and go,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.280
'Nah... Nah... Nah... Bra? Sorry, luv, no bras...'

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.980
'Trousers that make your arse look better... that one.'

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:19.820
Saw one for pasta, as well.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.180
We're actually looking in the bathroom for this.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.820
— Scales.
— Toilet.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.820
I like the idea that they put an insensitive in a scale.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:28.800
[Laughter]

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.020
'Step off, lardo. I can't take this any more.'

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.300
That's the old gag, though, isn't it?

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.840
You step on the scale, it says 'One at a time, please.'

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.780
'No coach parties.'

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:40.480
Bog roll!

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:42.580
GARY: That's a good place!

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:43.800
No, it's not, actually...

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.100
It's very obvious when you've run out of bog roll.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.340
But that answer's already got me points once before,

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.380
so I thought I'd try it again.
[Laughter]

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.000
Not this time.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.280
It's not absurd, though. Not in a private bathroom,

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.020
but in, like works ones,

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.020
— because you've got the automatic tap sensors...
— Yes.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.160
I've seen ones for, erm...

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.920
You're getting close with automatic tap sensors, by the way.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.700
Yeah. And there's the things where you give it that number,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.460
and it gives you a couple of sheets to dry your hands on,

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.300
so bog roll's just the next one on that stage.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:07.940
Yeah, but people want different amounts, don't they.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.320
You don't just want one or two sheets.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.820
Yeah, but you just go...
Toilets?

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:12.940
Everything in the bathroom apart from the one...

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:14.140
— You've got the water, the towels...
— Toilets! Showers!

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.900
— Soap.
— Bingo! Point.  [DING]

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.340
Oh yeah. [Mechanical noise]

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.100
Just going back to your bog roll thing...

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:22.300
Do you want to be sat in a work setting, in a cubicle,

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.420
and if you have to do this or something

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.180
to get the toilet roll to come out...

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.500
It'd be great for the Queen.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:28.460
[Laughter]
Yes.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.620
She'd never stop it. She'd just be surrounded by it.

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:31.760
[Laughter]
She'd be like an...

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.120
Slowly — just this hand, as the paper rises...!

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:36.940
It's actually the train on her wedding dress.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.140
It's winding around her hand...

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.320
'Nooo!'

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.640
She looks like a naughty Andrex puppy when they find her.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.480
But the other thing is, a toilet roll dispenser telling you —

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.360
like you say, in a public one — that there's not much left...

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.280
— 'Go careful now!'
[Laughter]

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.680
'If it was a curry, choose another stall.'

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.620
Isn't that what toilet attendants used to probably do?

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:58.660
Can you imagine?

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.020
'Not that one! She's out. Use Number 1.'

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.260
Can you tell me anywhere they might be sold?

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.640
We're looking for American retail stores...

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:06.720
Walmart!

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.800
Well, possibly, but we're looking for some more...

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.540
Bed Bath &amp; Beyond.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:12.640
— Point!
— Ohh!

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:13.900
— Damn.
— [DING] Spot on.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.440
I always did wonder what 'Beyond' was.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:17.800
Electric bins!

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:18.760
[Laughter]

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.840
Electric speaky clever bins,

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.840
and toilet roll warning devices.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.940
'Danger: Bog Roll.'

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:26.980
I want Danger Bog Roll.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.060
'Each sheet a new danger!'

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.700
— Cactus.
— 'This one is covered in crocodiles!'

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:33.920
Croc...!

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:35.600
[Gnashing sounds]

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.860
'There doesn't seem to be anything wrong with this one...

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:40.040
'Poison ivy!'

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:42.660
But why would I? She's lovely.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:43.660
[Groan]

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:46.600
No, just you've got Russian Roulette toilet paper.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.300
One's chili.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.680
Capsaicin, yeah.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.100
One sheet out of every six has poison ivy or capsaicin on it.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:54.240
Ohh, yes!

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.240
One out of six: spearmint.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:08:59.960
Tingly, but not necessarily wrong!

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.080
Mint sauce, kind of...

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:04.320
TCP.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.220
You'd smell that one from a mile off.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:07.640
Witch hazel.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.140
Tea tree!

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.980
That would spice up toilet breaks, wouldn't it though?

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:14.480
Russian Toilette.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:17.960
Yes!

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.160
Whoa, whoa, whoa! 'Wheel of Fart-une'.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:23.820
*Weal* of Fart-une.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:25.200
Yes.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.900
I've got in my head, Nicky Campbell...

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.440
Nicky Campbell!

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.660
Or Pat Sajak, for you Americans out there,

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.520
spinning the wheel, and you wonder what it'll land on.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.380
'I'm sorry, that's porcupine.'

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.620
'You've got... aloe vera.'

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:45.060
— 'Allo, Vera.'
— 'Alright, Gene.'

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.760
Ayyy!

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.180
Does anyone want to tell me
what Bed Bath &amp; Beyond used to be called?

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.840
Because the first one opened in 1971...
it had a different name.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.380
Is it something really prosaic, like Harrison's Home Supplies?

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.540
Sleep Health Privy &amp; Whatever.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.100
I'd say it's about one-third more prosaic.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:04.760
Bed &amp; Bath.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:05.980
Correct!  [DING]

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.580
'Wait, we're selling more than just beds and baths.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:12.060
'What do we call it?'

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.060
'Bed, Bath... "Beyond" will probably do.

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.580
'Everybody? Shall we knock off early?'

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.740
'Bed, Bath and... a third item.'

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.600
'Come in to see what it is!'

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:23.620
'I need some pillows, a new plug for the bath
and some Tarot cards.'

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:25.920
That was the second version of the name.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.100
It was just hanging off the edge of the store.

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:30.780
Bed Bath &amp; Tarot Cards?

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.700
Bed Bath Pillows Some Other Things &amp; Tarot Cards.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.360
While we're on Bed Bath &amp; Beyond, by the way...

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:37.460
since we seem to be going down this wiki-hole...

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.520
They've bought a lot of companies.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.660
Can you tell me what the name of the company

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.660
that sold Christmas trees was

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:45.580
that they bought about 2003?

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:46.760
Green Pricks!

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:49.940
What?

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:53.740
Chris Masterson's Christmas Trees.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.380
You're all being far too serious here.

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.220
The Christmas Tree Shop.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:10:59.840
— Point!  [DING]
— F*** off!

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:02.160
I was so close!

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.500
Ha! 'What do you sell?'
'Cars.'

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.620
All right. There is one last thing that simplehuman sell,

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.400
which is a sensor mirror.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:14.160
Can you tell me what it does?

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:15.800
Tells you you're ugly?

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.260
'Mirror, mirror on the wall...'

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.180
'How do I look today?'

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:23.200
Northerner mirror:
'F***in' dreadful.'

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.280
Is it a sensorless mirror?

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:30.800
Ha! A senseless mirror.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.280
'Idiot!'

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:33.900
A really insensitive —

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.200
'Why did you leave the house?!'

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.720
A sensor mirror? I mean, what, does it...

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:41.860
tell you you've brushed your teeth, or give you gestures...

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.000
I've got to be honest, all the sensors so far are just

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.800
if something is close to it. It's not much of a sensor.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.220
Does it turn the lights on?

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.440
Point.  [DING]

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.800
It turns the lights on if you're close to the mirror.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.400
How do you know you're close to the mirror

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.200
if the lights aren't on?

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:58.580
It doesn't turn the room lights on, it turns the mirror...

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.880
— Sensor mirror: Boomf!
— 'Oh f***!'

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.160
And you hammer the light switch,
which is just set a bit behind it.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.720
'I've sensed the mirror!'

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.720
'Why did I install this s****y mirror?'

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.900
'I've got to turn the lights off again now.'
Doof!

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.280
'Mirror, mirror, on the wa — ohhh!'

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:18.360
'Oh, you bastard!'

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.780
Same with the oven!

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:24.340
[Sizzling noise]
'Aaargh!'

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:28.600
At the end of that...

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.820
It's the most dangerous house going.

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.640
— The hot tap on the bath.
— Turn the telly on? Doonk!

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.520
[Groans]

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:44.040
'Honey? Will you switch on the light in the living room?'

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:48.000
I don't want to use the waste disposal.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:52.220
'I need to fry some bacon. Can you turn on the gas?'

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.140
Whoomph!

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:01.860
At the end of that, congratulations Matt!

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.260
— F***!
— What?!

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:05.460
You did score all the genuine points though.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:07.540
He did actually get the answers, yeah, you're right.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:10.520
You win a gift voucher to everyone's favourite
Communist high street shop,

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:12.780
which is Marx &amp; Spencer, so do enjoy that.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:16.900
Until then, that's been Matt Gray...

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:18.100
That's been Gary Brannan...

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:19.440
That's been Chris Joel.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.640
I've been Tom Scott. We'll see you next time.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:27.400
Hey, thanks for watching!

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:29.860
If you liked the show then tell someone, tell us,

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.160
or send us a telegram.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.900
And there are all-new episodes of our reverse trivia podcast

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:36.820
over at techdif.co.uk.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.720
